46. **One git history read per build (user, 2026-11-27).** docsRegistry step 6 made the real registry build run git three times per doc (~0.3s per git start on Windows → ~25s; observerCopy test timed out, sitemap test ~27s; the site build pays the same). User chose to read git history once per build over raising test timeouts. New shared module src/lib/utils/readFileHistory (approved addition to the #42 tree; src/lib/utils is the playbook's home for logic shared across libs, since one lib may not import a sibling lib) reads the whole history once per process (`git log -z --root -M --diff-merges=off --name-status`) and answers first-added (following renames), last-changed and last commit per file. resolveGitDates (keeps its contract) and the registry's resolveCommitHash both read from it. resolveGitDates spec gains the outcome "A build reads version history once, however many files it dates." Build order: readFileHistory spec → plan (user approval) → build; resolveGitDates step 2; docsRegistry step 7 (commit helper onto the reader, full suite with no timeout raised, closes step 5/6 checks); docsRegistry step 8 (registry test rewrite, formerly step 7); then structuredData, atomFeed, socialShare; then llmsTxt 2–5.
47. **Copies are not followed for first-added dates (user, 2026-11-27).** The one-per-build history reader counts a copied file as added on the day of the copy and does not follow it back. Parity against the old per-file lookup: 67 of 68 files identical; the one difference, `.claude/skills/aide/SKILL.md`, now dates 2026-05-09 (commit f1f6d3a, when it was actually added) instead of 2026-04-22 (the old `git log --follow` followed a 51% copy of `.claude/commands/aide.md`). Recorded in the readFileHistory and resolveGitDates plans.
